<BaseLayout
  title="Perturbing Inputs Improves Pretraining Distillation — Jack Cai"
  description="Distilling on perturbed inputs for part of pretraining improves small language models, and the gain comes from the teacher's predictions on the perturbed text."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>Perturbing Inputs Improves Pretraining Distillation</h1>
    <p>Ziyang Cai, Yihe Dong, Shivam Garg · October 2026 · <a class="pdf-link" href="/writing/input-perturbation-distillation.pdf">Paper (PDF)</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="post-body">
    <p>
      Knowledge distillation trains a student language model to match a teacher's predictions on the
      training text. We ask whether learning the teacher's behavior beyond the training text can further
      improve the student. We investigate this using three input perturbations: additive token noise,
      blending two documents, and blending neighbouring tokens. Specifically, for an early part of
      pretraining we perturb the inputs and train the student to match the teacher on them, and we spend
      the rest of the same token budget on ordinary distillation.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <a href="/writing/input-perturbation-overview.png"><img src="/writing/input-perturbation-overview.png" alt="Standard distillation, where teacher and student read clean text, next to the early stage of our method, where both read a perturbed input and the student matches the teacher on it." /></a>
    </figure>
    <p>
      When pretraining from scratch, the best method improves on standard distillation at both scales we
      tried, by 0.025 nats at 135M parameters and by 0.020 nats at 0.5B (three seeds). At 135M this is
      1.45 times the gain that distillation itself provides over next-token training. We also observe
      promising downstream transfer: at 0.5B, the best variant improves average zero-shot accuracy by
      about one percentage point across seven tasks. It also retains its validation-loss advantage after
      low-bit weight quantization. We also test a data-limited setting in which training data must be
      reused, and find that the gains persist. Finally, ablations suggest that, for additive noise,
      perturbing the teacher's inputs contributes most of the gain, while perturbing only the student's
      inputs yields much less.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <a href="/writing/input-perturbation-results.png"><img src="/writing/input-perturbation-results.png" alt="Change in final validation loss against standard distillation for each perturbation family at 135M and 0.5B." /></a>
      <figcaption>Change in final validation loss against standard distillation (below zero is better), at 135M (top) and 0.5B (bottom).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a class="pdf-link" href="/writing/input-perturbation-distillation.pdf">Read the full paper (PDF) →</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

